  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Octagonal cupola of Williamsburg’s historic schoolhouse stands sentinel over brick buildings that have witnessed centuries of American history unfold beneath Virginia skies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://familydestinationsguide.com/virginia-living-history-village/",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Stretching nearly a mile, this historic street is flanked by buildings that would make your history teacher weep—each meticulously restored to capture 18th-century architecture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://familydestinationsguide.com/virginia-living-history-village/",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Colonial Williamsburg isn’t just a tourist trap with actors in funny clothes—it’s 301 acres of immersive time travel that makes you wonder if you’ve driven through a wormhole.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://familydestinationsguide.com/virginia-living-history-village/",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "At the milliner’s shop, you’ll discover that fashion was just as important to colonial Virginians as it is to today’s Instagram influencers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://familydestinationsguide.com/virginia-living-history-village/",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Just down the street stands the Governor’s Palace, the most impressive residence in colonial Virginia and home to royal governors who represented the Crown’s authority.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://familydestinationsguide.com/virginia-living-history-village/",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Capitol building anchors the eastern end of Duke of Gloucester Street, standing proud as a symbol of Virginia’s colonial government and revolutionary spirit.",
